    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d522">

                <pb facs="0512" n="500" sameAs="frus:frus1939v01/pg500"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 European War 1939/25: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Rumania</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Gunther</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Bucharest</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1939-10-04T16:00:00+02:00">October 4, 1939—4
                            p.m.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 9:35 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>305. For the Secretary and President. My No. 296, October 2, 5 p.m. Prime
                    Minister Argetoianu has also asked me whether any <pb facs="0513" n="501"
                        xml:id="pg501"/> peace initiative is under contemplation. I told him I was
                    not informed but that speaking in a purely personal capacity such a move seemed
                    premature; anyway Germany’s condition has not yet been even officially outlined.
                    In reply to my queries regarding the background of his and Gafencu’s inquiries I
                    elicited the information that Von Papen<anchor n="74" xml:id="d522-f1"/> had
                    approached the Rumanian Ambassador at Ankara with the suggestion that the Baltic
                    States make a united peace movement. Argetoianu said the matter was under
                    consideration but that they wanted to hear from us first and then see whether
                    the Scandinavian, Baltic and other neutral states in Europe would act in unison.
                    He seemed to think that only we could determine England.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Gunther</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
